The dopamine feedback loop of likes, comments, and notifications exploits the same variable-ratio reinforcement schedule as slot machines — a design choice, not an accident, intended to maximise time-on-platform at the expense of user wellbeing.
Social comparison is a universal psychological mechanism; social media provides near-infinite upward comparison targets, which research consistently shows reduces satisfaction with one's own life.
Sean Parker, Facebook's founding president, explicitly stated in 2017 that the platform was designed to consume as much of users' time and conscious attention as possible — admitting the design goal conflicts with user wellbeing.
